1. Executive Summary: Narrow Single-Department Pilot Unlocks
Faster MRD Chart Closure and Lower Claim Denials
1.1 Problem: Post-Discharge Manual Checks Create Billing Bottlenecks and
Accreditation Risk
In medium-to-large secondary and tertiary care hospitals across India, every inpatient case sheet
arriving in the Hospital Medical Records Department (MRD) after physical patient discharge must pass
two sequential, largely paper- or scan-based manual reviews before final archiving and insurance claim
submission: (1) a completeness check verifying physician signatures, discharge summary presence,
operative notes, consent forms, and mandatory fields required under National Accreditation Board for
Hospitals &amp; Healthcare Providers (NABH) standards, and (2) a clinical coding check translating free-text
diagnostic and procedural narratives into standardized World Health Organization ICD-10 codes. Today,
this workflow suffers from four severe operational failure modes:
       Slow and inconsistent ICD-10 coding (15 to 30+ minutes per complex chart): Because clinical notes
contain unstandardized physician abbreviations, specialty-specific shorthand, and multi-morbidity
narratives, two coding officers reviewing similar surgical charts frequently assign divergent CDC ICD-
10-CM codes. Inaccurate or unspecified codes directly trigger Third-Party Administrator (TPA) and
insurance claim rejections (MediCodio AI).
     Labor-intensive deficiency chasing across wards: MRD Deficiency Officers manually flip through
multi-page scanned or physical files to spot missing signatures or incomplete discharge advice, then
chase busy clinicians via one-on-one phone calls or physical ward rounds—a repetitive process
prone to human oversight and multi-day delays (PMC Audit Study).
    Blind First-In-First-Out (FIFO) queue processing: All discharge records sit in an undifferentiated
queue regardless of financial claim value, TPA submission deadline, or regulatory audit sensitivity,
causing high-value cashless/reimbursement files and high-audit-risk records to stall behind routine
low-complexity cash charts.
     Downstream hospital-wide ripple effects: MRD backlogs delay insurance cash flow, stall medico-
legal record issuances, impede statutory communicable disease and mortality reporting, and expose
the hospital to non-compliance citations during NABH 6th Edition and Joint Commission
International (JCI) surveillance audits.
